- tape recorders and hi-fi systems improve;
- electronic telephone switching spreads;
- calculators appear in laboratories, banks, and engineering offices;
- early business computers become common in large firms.

## 1950s–1960s
Integrated circuits likely emerge earlier. Hybrid ICs could appear in the 1940s; monolithic silicon ICs by the late 1940s or 1950s are plausible if semiconductor manufacturing advances aggressively.

Then:
- minicomputers appear in the 1950s;
- scientific computing expands rapidly;
- process control spreads through chemicals, steel, oil refining, electricity, and aviation;
- early video games and electronic music synthesizers appear earlier;
- digital watches and pocket calculators arrive earlier.

## 1960s–1970s
With earlier ICs, microprocessors could appear in the late 1950s or 1960s rather than 1971.

By 1980, rich countries might have:
- home computers in many middle-class households;
- computer terminals in schools and offices;
- public data networks;
- email-like messaging among universities, firms, and governments;
- electronic banking and ATMs;
- barcode-based retail logistics;
- early mobile phones, especially car phones and professional handhelds;
- computer-controlled appliances;
- electronic engine control in cars;
- advanced medical monitors and pacemakers;
- satellite TV and global communications.

It probably is not a smartphone world by 1980. Batteries, flat-panel displays, wireless networking, and software ecosystems are still bottlenecks. But it could look closer to the actual early 1990s in computing and networking than to the actual 1980.

