# 11. How different the world might feel in 1980

By 1980, this alternate world would not necessarily look like 2020, but in electronics it could resemble our **mid-to-late 1990s**.

A plausible 1980:

- transistor radios have been common for decades;
- color TV is mature;
- portable video cameras exist;
- personal computers are entering homes and offices;
- cellular phones are emerging;
- digital banking and electronic funds transfer are common in advanced economies;
- packet-switched networks connect universities, corporations, and militaries;
- satellite communications and reconnaissance are routine;
- semiconductor manufacturing is a core strategic industry;
- Japan and Germany are major electronics competitors;
- the Soviet Union has advanced military electronics but lags badly in consumer computing;
- privacy, surveillance, environmental pollution, and cyber risk are already political issues.

The biggest difference from our 1980 is not that everything is magical. It is that **the digital infrastructure of the late 20th century has been building since the 1940s**, giving societies more time to adapt, compete, regulate, and militarize electronics.

